\DOC LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Steps an {eventually}-style machine-code goal across one inlined
Montgomery-multiplication subroutine call, advancing the symbolic state
variable and replacing the state-dependent assumptions with ones about the new
state, including a conditional characterisation of the newly written result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which of the subroutine's
operand/pointer-setup variants is being traversed; it governs how many extra
register-setup assumptions (such as an argument pointer register read) appear
in the resulting goal. The second argument is a {string list} of three names
identifying the destination and the two source buffers of the multiplication:
the first supplies the fresh variable standing for the computed result, the
other two the terms it is said to be built from. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is {eventually ... s} for a current state variable {s}, the tactic
produces the same predicate applied to a later state variable {s'}. Assumptions
not mentioning the state (address disjointness, bounds and modular facts about
earlier intermediate values) are kept unchanged. Every assumption mentioning
{s} — the program-counter read, the code-loaded fact, the register and
memory-content reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — is discharged
and re-asserted for {s'}, with the program counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE}
relation extended by the memory region just written. Two assumptions are new:
one recording that this region of {s'} holds the fresh result variable, and an
implication bounding the product and equating the result to the Montgomery
product of the two source values modulo the prime. Some applications
additionally add a register read assumption not present before.
{
   A, (assumptions mentioning s) ?- eventually ... s
   ================================================  LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC n ["dest"; "src1"; "src2"]
   A, (same assumptions re-asserted for s', pc advanced, MAYCHANGE extended),
      (region of s' holds fresh result variable),
      (implication bounding the product and equating the result to the
       Montgomery product of the two source values modulo the prime),
      (possibly an extra register read assumption)
    ?- eventually ... s'
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this kind of forward-stepping transformation
appears, always on goals of this {eventually} machine-state form, and each
produces exactly one subgoal.

\ENDDOC
